[{"id":"1783251138689-TD_Recursion.pdf-0","docId":"1783251138689-TD_Recursion.pdf","chunkIndex":0,"text":"Exercise 1 — Factorial \nConsider the following method: \npublic static int factorial(int n) { \n    if (n < 0) \n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(); \n    else if (n == 0) \n        return 1; \n    else \n        return n * factorial(n-1); \n} \n1. What is the base case of this recursion? \n2. What is the recursive case? \n3. Draw the recursive call tree for factorial(5). \n4. What value is returned by factorial(5)? \n \nExercise 2 — Sum of Array Elements \nWrite a recursive method that returns the sum of the first n elements of the array using \nrecursion. \n \nExercise 3 — Binary Search (Recursive) \n \na. Write a recursive method binarySearch that searches for a target value in a \nsorted array and returns true if the element exists; otherwise, return false. \nb. Write a non-recursive version of binary search \nc. Compare complexities of the two versions of the binary search. \nExercise 4 — Reverse an Array \nWrite a recursive method reverseArray that reverses the elements of the array. \nP.S. :","embedding":[0.029642464593052864,0.03570489212870598,-0.013511433266103268,-0.04967261105775833,-0.0745578408241272,-0.04328544810414314,0.004150231834501028,0.013716484420001507,-0.07031641900539398,0.07334534078836441,-0.0736071914434433,-0.007544836029410362,0.010107669048011303,-0.059785451740026474,-0.07861030101776123,-0.0319269560277462,-0.015821823850274086,0.021844875067472458,0.07102025300264359,-0.036607757210731506,0.033913273364305496,-0.06184478849172592,-0.03312985226511955,-0.05161953344941139,-0.01061063352972269,0.06023367866873741,-0.06596662104129791,0.006419983226805925,0.021674757823348045,-0.03123537078499794,-0.017630504444241524,0.08767427504062653,0.07335828989744186,-0.013119005598127842,-0.04576166719198227,0.0847974419593811,0.0039121294394135475,-0.05809682980179787,-0.032041843980550766,0.08250696957111359,-0.085319414734840